ftrace: skip the boot-time weak-function scan when sorttable has done it

ftrace_check_for_weak_functions() queues a scan of every mcount record
through kallsyms_lookup() to disable the records of overridden weak
functions, and ftrace_check_sync() waits for it at late_initcall_sync. Since
6.15 sorttable zeroes those records at build time (BUILDTIME_MCOUNT_SORT),
and ftrace_process_locs() skips what it zeroed, so the scan finds nothing.
Module records are still checked where they are loaded.

Measured 2026-09-29 on the lab runner: the scan is a kworker's 11.9 ms of CPU
during boot, which on one vCPU runs inside acpi_init; with this patch and
0001, kernel boot 110.0 -> 89.5 ms, and available_filter_functions lists no
__ftrace_invalid_address__ records.

Not upstream: the scan is unconditional in master (7.3-rc5).

--- a/kernel/trace/ftrace.c
+++ b/kernel/trace/ftrace.c
@@ -4405,6 +4405,14 @@
 
 static int __init ftrace_check_for_weak_functions(void)
 {
+	/*
+	 * sorttable zeroes the mcount entries of overridden weak functions at build
+	 * time, and ftrace_process_locs() skips what it zeroed: there is nothing left
+	 * for this scan of every record through kallsyms to find.
+	 */
+	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_BUILDTIME_MCOUNT_SORT))
+		return 0;
+
 	INIT_WORK(&ftrace_check_work, ftrace_check_work_func);
 
 	ftrace_check_wq = alloc_workqueue("ftrace_check_wq", WQ_UNBOUND, 0);
